{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the given recurrence relation \\(a_n = 2a_{n-1} - 2a_{n-2} + a_{n-3}\\), we start by finding the characteristic equation of the recurrence relation. The characteristic equation is obtained by assuming a solution of the form \\(a_n = r^n\\). Substituting \\(a_n = r^n\\) into the recurrence relation gives:\n\\[\nr^n = 2r^{n-1} - 2r^{n-2} + r^{n-3}.\n\\]\nDividing through by \\(r^{n-3}\\) (assuming \\(r \\neq 0\\)), we get:\n\\[\nr^3 = 2r^2 - 2r + 1.\n\\]\nRearranging terms, we obtain the characteristic equation:\n\\[\nr^3 - 2r^2 + 2r - 1 = 0.\n\\]\nWe can factor this polynomial as:\n\\[\n(r-1)(r^2 - r + 1) = 0.\n\\]\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are \\(r = 1\\) and the roots of the quadratic equation \\(r^2 - r + 1 = 0\\). The roots of \\(r^2 - r + 1 = 0\\) are:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1-4}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\nThus, the roots are \\(r = 1\\), \\(r = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\), and \\(r = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). The general solution to the recurrence relation is:\n\\[\na_n = A \\cdot 1^n + B \\left( \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)^n + C \\left( \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)^n.\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\na_n = A + B \\left( \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)^n + C \\left( \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)^n.\n\\]\nWe know that \\(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) and \\(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) are the complex cube roots of unity, so \\(\\left( \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)^3 = 1\\) and \\(\\left( \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)^3 = 1\\). This means that the terms involving these roots are periodic with period 3. Therefore, we can write:\n\\[\na_n = A + B \\omega^n + C \\omega^{2n},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\omega = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) and \\(\\omega^2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n\n","prefix_end":1688,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":